course: everyday
lesson: 473
title: The ball over the fence and the grumpy old man next door
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sam's football flies over the fence into Mr Grimshaw's garden. Mr Grimshaw comes out holding it like a dead rat: «That's the third this month. You'll get them back at Christmas.» Behind him, his tomato plants are flattened. He even shouts at kids for walking on the edge of his lawn, «Off the grass! Do you live here? No!», so everyone calls him «Grumpy Grimshaw». Grumpy, yes, but he has a reason. Sam's dad goes round, Sam says sorry about the tomatoes, and they agree: the kids play at the far end and knock to ask, and Mr Grimshaw hands balls back.
explain: One icy morning Sam sees Mr Grimshaw wobbling up his slippery path with four bags of shopping. Sam takes two. Not a word all the way, then at the door: «Thank you, lad.» Sam's mum tells him Mr Grimshaw's wife died last year and he lives alone. After that the balls come back straight away, sometimes with a toffee. «He's actually alright, y'know,» says Kieran, chewing. By August Sam has grown a huge red tomato from Mr Grimshaw's seeds.
explain: Not every neighbour softens. Mrs Platt at number 22 shuts her curtains when kids say hello and rings the council about hopscotch, so the kids play in the park instead. Some kids shout «old witch» at her through the letterbox; she tells their parents, and they're grounded for a week. Mr Hal, who sits on his porch at number 9, says «kids these days» at everyone, until Mia draws him a picture of his old dog — now he waves whenever she passes. Maddie's ball gets chewed to shreds by the neighbour's Staffie (a Staffordshire bull terrier, a strong, stocky dog); «Tough luck, love.» Her mum buys a new one: «Some folk are like that, pet. Play at the front.»
explain: Mistakes get worse when you run or climb. Jake climbs Mr Grimshaw's fence and smashes a pot; he pays for it from three weeks' pocket money. Connor's ball breaks the greenhouse glass, and he legs it (runs off) and hides under his bed. His mum marches him back. Mr Grimshaw is very cross, then says, «At least you came back. That's more than most.» Lily's grandad says, «Some folk have had hard lives, love. Grumpy's how they cope.»
fact: Neighbours who seem grumpy often have a reason, such as damaged plants, tiredness or loneliness.
fact: When your ball goes into someone's garden, the right thing is to knock and ask, not to climb in.
fact: Climbing fences can break things in the garden and you can get hurt.
fact: A real apology and a simple agreement can turn a quarrel with a neighbour into peace.
fact: Many older people live alone, and loneliness can make a person seem cross.
fact: A small kind act, like carrying shopping or drawing a picture, can soften a grumpy person.
fact: Some people stay grumpy whatever you do; then it is fine to play somewhere else.
fact: If you break something by accident, owning up is better than running away; running away makes you look guiltier.
fact: Shouting rude words back at a cross neighbour makes things worse; walk away and tell your parents.
fact: Understanding why someone is grumpy helps you stay patient, even if they never change.
fact: «Legs it» is British slang for runs away fast; «pet», «love» and «chuck» are friendly words for «dear».
exercise: Why was Mr Grimshaw cross about the football? | It was the third one and it flattened his tomatoes | He hates all children for no reason, and always has done | The ball was his and Sam stole it | The ball broke his front door
exercise: What did Sam's dad and Mr Grimshaw agree? | Kids play at the far end and knock to ask | Kids never play football in the street again | Mr Grimshaw keeps every ball forever | Sam pays for a new garden
exercise: How did Sam help Mr Grimshaw one icy morning? | He carried his shopping bags | He gritted the whole street with salt | He called an ambulance for him | He made him a hot cup of tea
exercise: What did Sam's mum tell him about Mr Grimshaw? | His wife had died and he lived alone | He used to be a famous footballer for Leeds | He was moving away soon | He had ten grandchildren nearby
exercise: After the shopping, how did Mr Grimshaw treat the balls? | He gave them back at once, sometimes with a toffee | He started popping them with a garden fork | He threw them back over at the kids | He kept them all in his shed till Christmas
exercise: Which neighbour stayed grumpy no matter what? | Mrs Platt at number 22 | Mr Grimshaw next door | Mr Hal on the porch | Mr Patel who runs the shop
exercise: What did the kids do when Mrs Platt kept complaining? | Moved their games to the park | Shouted at her every day | Threw balls at her front window | Called the police on her
exercise: Maddie's ball got chewed by the neighbour's dog. What did her mum say? | Some folk are like that, play at the front | Go round and shout at the neighbour about it | Climb over and get the dog | Never play with a ball again
exercise: Jake climbed the fence without asking. What happened? | He smashed a pot and had to pay for it | He found ten lost balls in the flower beds | Mr Grimshaw gave him a prize | Nothing, nobody saw him
exercise: How did Mia make friends with Mr Hal? | She drew him a picture | She fixed the roof of his porch | She gave him all her pocket money | She told him off for moaning so much
exercise: Connor broke the greenhouse and hid. What did Mr Grimshaw say when he came back? | «At least you came back» | «Never come near me again» | «I'm calling the police» | «It doesn't matter at all»
exercise: What did Mr Grimshaw teach Sam? | How to grow tomatoes from seed | How to fix burst footballs with tape | How to climb fences safely | How to build a greenhouse
exercise: The kids shouted «old witch» at a neighbour. What happened? | She told their parents and they were grounded | She gave them sweets to make them stop | She moved house the next day | Nothing, it was funny
exercise: According to Lily's grandad, why are some people grumpy? | They've had hard lives and it's how they cope | They were born grumpy and can't ever change at all | They are always secretly happy | They want children to move away
exercise: Why did the kids call him «Grumpy Grimshaw»? | He shouted at them for stepping on his lawn | He never once spoke to anyone on the street, not even hello | He looked like a cartoon villain | He stole their bikes
exercise: What does «legs it» mean? | Runs away fast | Walks very slowly | Kicks a ball | Climbs a fence
test: «ball's gone over Grimshaw's again, just hop the fence, he won't know». Better idea? | Knock and ask for it back | Hop over quickly while he naps | Send the smallest kid over | Kick another ball to knock it out
test: «Grumpy Grimshaw just hates kids, simple». Is it that simple? | No, he was lonely and his plants got wrecked | Yes, some people just hate all kids, full stop | Yes, all old people are grumpy | No, he secretly owns a sweet shop
test: Connor: «I smashed his greenhouse and legged it, if I stay hid nobody'll know». What should he do? | Go back, own up and say sorry | Stay under the bed till it's forgotten | Blame Kieran for it | Break another pane so it looks like wind
test: What would probably happen if the kids kept shouting «old witch» at the neighbour? | More trouble with her and their parents | She'd become their best friend | She'd give them a big apology | The council would give them a prize for it
test: «mrs platt's well nesh, moanin at the wind chimes». («nesh» in the North means easily bothered by cold.) The kids are kind but she stays grumpy. What's sensible? | Accept it and play somewhere else | Keep annoying her till she smiles | Ring her doorbell and run | Break her wind chimes
test: «why'd Sam carry the shopping for HIM, after he nicked our balls?» What's the best answer? | A small kindness can crack a grumpy person | Sam wanted to steal the toffees from his kitchen | Sam was paid a lot for it | Sam was scared of Mr Grimshaw
test: Mia's mum says, «Mr Hal says 'kids these days' at everyone, love.» What did Mia's picture show? | Little gifts can open doors | Drawing makes people move away | Pictures make people angrier | Mr Hal wanted money, not pictures
test: In a cartoon, a grumpy neighbour is just evil and gets a pie in the face. What's more often true? | Grumpy people often have hard lives or are lonely | Grumpy neighbours are always secret villains | Pies fix grumpy people | Grumpy people never change
test: Jake says, «it was only a cheap pot, why should I pay?» Fair to pay? | Yes, he broke it | No, cheap things like that don't count | No, Mr Grimshaw should pay for it | Yes, but only if it's over £100
test: Kieran: «he gives us toffees now, he's proper sound». What changed Mr Grimshaw? | Kindness and an honest sorry | The kids threatened him over the fence | He won the lottery and cheered up | Nothing, he was always like that
exam: Identify the principal reason, as established in the lesson, for the initial hostility of the neighbour Mr Grimshaw towards the children. | Repeated damage to his tomato plants by footballs | An inherent dislike of all young persons | A dispute concerning the ownership of a fence | A complaint from the municipal council
exam: Ascertain the most appropriate conduct of a child whose ball has entered a neighbouring garden. | To knock at the neighbour's door and request its return | To climb the boundary fence without permission and retrieve it | To abandon the ball permanently | To send a younger child to retrieve it secretly
exam: Given that a child has accidentally broken a pane of a neighbour's greenhouse, select the response consistent with the established facts. | To return, confess and apologise is preferable to concealment | Concealment beneath one's bed resolves the matter | Flight renders the child less likely to be suspected | The neighbour bears responsibility for the breakage
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a neighbour who remains disagreeable notwithstanding repeated kindness? | It is acceptable to accept the situation and play elsewhere | Children are obliged to continue until she relents | She ought to be subjected to retaliation | Her conduct proves that kindness is always futile
